INT. ABANDONED CIRCUS - RINGMASTER'S WAGON - AFTERNOON

A painted wagon, faded to the color of old peaches, its walls hung with moth-eaten posters of lions and strongmen. A cracked mirror. A brass bed with a mangy leopard-print throw. Afternoon sun slants through shuttered windows, and dust turns in the beams.

ARNOLD "THE MAGNIFICENT" PELLEGRINO, seventy, in a satin dressing gown and an immense waxed mustache, sits before the mirror applying greasepaint to a face that has long ago stopped taking it. He hums, off key.

The door creaks. He does not turn.

ARNOLD
If you're the bailiff, I'm indisposed. If you're the ghost, you're late.

The door opens fully. A young woman steps in with a briefcase and a paper bag of sandwiches, blinking in the dimness. This is CORINNE WACHOWIAK-BELL, twenty-eight, a real estate appraiser, in a gray pencil skirt and sensible shoes, and she is clearly the most out of place person who has ever entered this wagon.

CORINNE
Mr. Pellegrino?

ARNOLD
Arnold the Magnificent.

CORINNE
I'm Corinne Wachowiak-Bell. I'm here about the lot.

ARNOLD
I know why you're here, my dear.

CORINNE
I brought sandwiches.

ARNOLD
I never eat before a show.

CORINNE
There's no show.

ARNOLD
There is always a show.

She sets the sandwiches on the bed. The leopard throw shifts and a cat's head pops out. She jumps.

CORINNE
You have a cat.

ARNOLD
I have a leopard.

CORINNE
That's a cat.

ARNOLD
She is thirteen years old and has eaten two assistants. Be kind.

Corinne laughs, then stops, then laughs again, nervously. She takes a step toward the mirror. In it, her reflection stands beside his.

CORINNE
Mr. Pellegrino, the county wants to demolish the grounds. They sent me to appraise the equipment.

ARNOLD
The equipment.

CORINNE
The wagons, the tent, the cannon.

ARNOLD
The cannon is not equipment. The cannon is a colleague.

CORINNE
I'll need to measure it.

ARNOLD
Measure her gently.

CORINNE
I will.

ARNOLD
You have kind hands. I can tell.

CORINNE
You can't tell that from the mirror.

ARNOLD
Forty years in the business, child. Nobody lies to the mirror.

He swivels on his stool at last and looks at her. His eyes are very blue under the greasepaint.

ARNOLD (CONT'D)
Sit.

CORINNE
I don't have a lot of time.

ARNOLD
Nobody does. Sit.

She sits on the edge of the brass bed. The leopard stares at her. She stares back.

ARNOLD (CONT'D)
When I was twenty-two, I swallowed fire in this very ring. Two thousand people on the benches. I breathed in, and for one second I forgot to be afraid. You know what that felt like?

CORINNE
Like winning?

ARNOLD
Like nothing. Like the moment before the heartbeat. That is what a circus is, Miss Wachowiak-Bell. A very expensive way to hold your breath.

CORINNE
And now?

ARNOLD
Now the breath has run out.

Corinne looks at her briefcase. She looks at the posters. She pulls out a clipboard and sets it on her knee.

CORINNE
Mr. Pellegrino, I'm going to be honest with you. This is not the kind of job I like.

ARNOLD
No?

CORINNE
No. I like houses. Kitchens, with granite. I like when somebody's moving in.

ARNOLD
Then why did you take this one?

CORINNE
They didn't give me a choice.

ARNOLD
That is the circus. They never do.

A beat. She smiles. He winks. The leopard rolls onto its back.

Outside, an engine coughs awake and a chain rattles against iron. Corinne stands. Arnold does not.

CORINNE
That'll be the cannon.

ARNOLD
(to himself)
So it begins.
